1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,747
Enron wasn’t destroyed because
it ran out of customers.

2
00:00:03,997 --> 00:00:07,457
In 2001, its pipelines
were still moving gas.

3
00:00:07,497 --> 00:00:09,763
Its traders were still making deals.

4
00:00:09,803 --> 00:00:13,117
The real problem had started
almost a decade earlier,

5
00:00:13,157 --> 00:00:15,506
with a decision about how to count money.

6
00:00:15,546 --> 00:00:19,731
In 1992, Enron got permission
to use an accounting method

7
00:00:19,771 --> 00:00:22,556
that could turn profits
expected in the future...

8
00:00:22,596 --> 00:00:24,651
into profits reported today.

9
00:00:24,691 --> 00:00:26,686
The cash didn’t have to arrive yet.

10
00:00:26,726 --> 00:00:29,582
But on paper,
Enron could look spectacular.

11
00:00:29,882 --> 00:00:32,397
And as Wall Street fell
in love with the company,

12
00:00:32,437 --> 00:00:35,236
keeping that picture
alive became critical.

13
00:00:35,276 --> 00:00:39,222
By 2000, Enron was worth
nearly $70 billion.

14
00:00:39,262 --> 00:00:43,589
Then, in the fall of 2001,
it collapsed in less than two months.

15
00:00:44,139 --> 00:00:46,963
Billions in savings. Thousands of jobs.

16
00:00:47,003 --> 00:00:49,592
One of America’s most admired companies.

17
00:00:49,632 --> 00:00:54,006
To understand how, you need one
accounting idea that changed everything.

18
00:00:57,006 --> 00:00:59,487
Houston, Texas. 1985.

19
00:00:59,527 --> 00:01:01,849
Two natural gas pipeline companies merge

20
00:01:01,889 --> 00:01:05,410
into what soon becomes Enron,
led by Kenneth Lay.

21
00:01:05,450 --> 00:01:07,709
At first, Enron moves gas. Steady.

22
00:01:07,749 --> 00:01:09,591
Predictable. A little boring.

23
00:01:09,631 --> 00:01:13,812
Then Lay brings in a sharp McKinsey
consultant named Jeffrey Skilling.

24
00:01:13,852 --> 00:01:16,487
Skilling’s big idea was a “Gas Bank.”

25
00:01:16,527 --> 00:01:18,437
Enron wouldn’t just move energy.

26
00:01:18,477 --> 00:01:21,027
It would trade it,
the way a bank trades money.

27
00:01:21,067 --> 00:01:24,714
It worked. By the late ’90s,
Enron was trading natural gas,

28
00:01:24,754 --> 00:01:27,268
electricity, even internet bandwidth.

29
00:01:27,308 --> 00:01:29,518
Fortune magazine named it “America’s

30
00:01:29,558 --> 00:01:32,428
Most Innovative Company”
six years in a row.

31
00:01:32,468 --> 00:01:37,335
In 2000, Enron reported
revenue of about $100 billion.

32
00:01:37,375 --> 00:01:40,310
That August, its stock
hit an all-time high.

33
00:01:40,350 --> 00:01:42,451
More than $90 a share.

34
00:01:42,491 --> 00:01:44,043
Enron looked like the future.

35
00:01:44,083 --> 00:01:48,062
But underneath that success was a
question almost nobody was asking.

36
00:01:48,102 --> 00:01:51,226
How much of that profit had
actually arrived as cash?

37
00:01:53,277 --> 00:01:56,426
To answer that,
we have to go back to 1992.

38
00:01:56,466 --> 00:01:59,839
Skilling wanted Enron to use
mark-to-market accounting.

39
00:01:59,879 --> 00:02:03,802
In January 1992, the Securities
and Exchange Commission

40
00:02:03,842 --> 00:02:07,118
approved it for Enron’s
natural gas contracts.

41
00:02:07,158 --> 00:02:09,836
Imagine Enron signs a 10-year contract

42
00:02:09,876 --> 00:02:13,189
it expects to earn $100 million in profit.

43
00:02:13,229 --> 00:02:16,783
Under traditional accounting,
that profit shows up gradually,

44
00:02:16,823 --> 00:02:19,720
as the gas is delivered
and the money comes in.

45
00:02:19,760 --> 00:02:20,848
Under mark-to-market,

46
00:02:20,888 --> 00:02:24,310
Enron could estimate what the
whole contract was worth today,

47
00:02:24,350 --> 00:02:26,722
and book that value as profit now.

48
00:02:26,762 --> 00:02:28,704
Year one. One giant number.

49
00:02:29,229 --> 00:02:31,239
Now, mark-to-market isn’t fraud.

50
00:02:31,279 --> 00:02:34,563
It’s widely used for things
with clear market prices.

51
00:02:34,603 --> 00:02:37,028
The danger is in one word. Estimate.

52
00:02:37,451 --> 00:02:41,761
Many of Enron’s long-term deals had
no market price to check against.

53
00:02:41,801 --> 00:02:44,819
Their value rested on
Enron’s own assumptions.

54
00:02:44,859 --> 00:02:48,756
Make those assumptions a little more
optimistic, and the profit gets bigger.

55
00:02:48,796 --> 00:02:49,823
Instantly.

56
00:02:49,863 --> 00:02:53,347
And here’s the crucial part.
The profit is reported now.

57
00:02:53,387 --> 00:02:55,297
The cash might arrive years later.

58
00:02:55,337 --> 00:02:57,653
Or never match the estimate at all.

59
00:02:58,061 --> 00:03:01,474
Accounting profit is what the
financial statements say you earned.

60
00:03:01,514 --> 00:03:04,993
Cash flow is the money that
actually comes through the door.

61
00:03:05,033 --> 00:03:07,342
At Enron, the two drifted far apart.

62
00:03:07,382 --> 00:03:11,967
One real example: in July 2000,
Enron announced a 20-year deal

63
00:03:12,007 --> 00:03:14,543
with Blockbuster for movies on demand.

64
00:03:14,583 --> 00:03:19,494
It never got beyond small pilot tests,
and it ended in March 2001.

65
00:03:19,534 --> 00:03:24,045
But Enron had already booked
about $111 million in profit

66
00:03:24,085 --> 00:03:27,838
from it, based largely on
projected future earnings.

67
00:03:28,438 --> 00:03:31,743
And once you’ve reported a
contract’s future profit today,

68
00:03:31,783 --> 00:03:33,992
you can’t report it again tomorrow.

69
00:03:34,032 --> 00:03:36,705
So to keep growing,
Enron needed more deals.

70
00:03:36,745 --> 00:03:39,044
Bigger deals. Every single quarter.

71
00:03:39,084 --> 00:03:40,914
The accounting had built a treadmill.

72
00:03:40,954 --> 00:03:42,468
And it was speeding up.

73
00:03:44,524 --> 00:03:48,390
In 1999, Enron’s stock rose 56%.

74
00:03:48,430 --> 00:03:51,533
In 2000, another 87%.

75
00:03:51,573 --> 00:03:54,995
Stock tickers were installed
in the lobbies and elevators.

76
00:03:55,035 --> 00:03:58,056
Executives were paid
heavily in stock options.

77
00:03:58,096 --> 00:03:59,944
The share price wasn’t just a number.

78
00:03:59,984 --> 00:04:01,548
It was the scoreboard.

79
00:04:01,588 --> 00:04:04,998
Analysts expected Enron to
hit its targets every quarter.

80
00:04:05,038 --> 00:04:07,997
And Enron delivered. Again. And again.

81
00:04:08,037 --> 00:04:10,875
But every success raised
the bar for the next one.

82
00:04:10,915 --> 00:04:12,813
Meanwhile, some of its biggest bets,

83
00:04:12,853 --> 00:04:16,730
like overseas power plants and a
broadband network, were struggling.

84
00:04:16,770 --> 00:04:18,816
The machine needed constant growth.

85
00:04:18,856 --> 00:04:21,557
And real growth was
getting harder to find.

86
00:04:21,597 --> 00:04:23,619
So where do you put the
losses, and the debt,

87
00:04:23,659 --> 00:04:25,651
when your story can’t afford them?

88
00:04:27,701 --> 00:04:32,386
Enter Andrew Fastow, Enron’s
chief financial officer from 1998.

89
00:04:32,426 --> 00:04:34,814
His specialty: special-purpose entities.

90
00:04:34,854 --> 00:04:38,766
Basically, a separate company
created for one specific job.

91
00:04:38,806 --> 00:04:41,668
Companies use them
legitimately all the time.

92
00:04:41,708 --> 00:04:45,912
Under the rules back then,
an outside investor with as little as 3%

93
00:04:45,952 --> 00:04:50,137
of the capital at risk could make
the entity count as independent.

94
00:04:50,177 --> 00:04:51,057
And that mattered.

95
00:04:51,097 --> 00:04:55,048
Because its assets, and its debt,
could stay off Enron’s balance sheet.

96
00:04:55,088 --> 00:04:57,348
Enron used them again and again.

97
00:04:57,388 --> 00:05:02,712
Chewco. LJM. And the Raptors, named
after the dinosaurs in Jurassic Park.

98
00:05:02,752 --> 00:05:06,568
But in several cases, the outside
money wasn’t really outside.

99
00:05:06,608 --> 00:05:10,445
Chewco, it later emerged,
didn’t meet the 3% rule at all.

100
00:05:10,745 --> 00:05:14,151
The Raptors made Enron’s
investment losses look hedged,

101
00:05:14,191 --> 00:05:15,765
covered by someone else.

102
00:05:15,805 --> 00:05:19,801
But that someone else was effectively
backed by Enron’s own stock.

103
00:05:19,841 --> 00:05:23,901
A later investigation for Enron’s
board found these deals inflated

104
00:05:23,941 --> 00:05:28,696
reported earnings by almost $1
billion, in just over a year.

105
00:05:29,296 --> 00:05:31,320
Then there was the conflict of interest.

106
00:05:31,360 --> 00:05:34,696
Fastow didn’t just design
the LJM partnerships.

107
00:05:34,736 --> 00:05:37,208
He ran them, while serving as CFO.

108
00:05:37,248 --> 00:05:41,649
Enron’s board approved it, as an
exception to its own code of conduct.

109
00:05:41,689 --> 00:05:46,369
The investigation found Fastow was
involved on both sides of these deals,

110
00:05:46,409 --> 00:05:49,312
and made more than $30 million from them.

111
00:05:49,812 --> 00:05:52,229
On paper, Enron looked stronger than ever.

112
00:05:52,269 --> 00:05:56,410
In reality, risk was piling up in
places investors could barely see.

113
00:05:58,460 --> 00:06:00,348
But some people started looking.

114
00:06:00,388 --> 00:06:02,060
March 2001.

115
00:06:02,100 --> 00:06:05,923
In Fortune, the same magazine that
kept calling Enron innovative,

116
00:06:05,963 --> 00:06:08,123
reporter Bethany McLean asked:

117
00:06:08,163 --> 00:06:09,988
“Is Enron Overpriced?”

118
00:06:10,488 --> 00:06:14,033
The real question: how exactly
does Enron make its money?

119
00:06:14,073 --> 00:06:17,598
The financial statements were
nearly impossible to decode.

120
00:06:17,638 --> 00:06:21,797
The debt was growing. And the cash
didn’t seem to line up with the profits.

121
00:06:21,837 --> 00:06:24,879
In August, just six
months after becoming CEO,

122
00:06:24,919 --> 00:06:28,120
Jeffrey Skilling resigned,
citing personal reasons.

123
00:06:28,160 --> 00:06:30,975
Kenneth Lay took the job back,
and assured the market:

124
00:06:31,015 --> 00:06:33,419
no change in performance or outlook.

125
00:06:33,459 --> 00:06:35,578
The next day, a vice
president named Sherron

126
00:06:35,618 --> 00:06:38,559
Watkins sent Lay an anonymous letter.

127
00:06:38,599 --> 00:06:40,412
One line would become famous.

128
00:06:40,812 --> 00:06:43,470
“I am incredibly nervous
that we will implode

129
00:06:43,510 --> 00:06:45,817
in a wave of accounting scandals.”

130
00:06:46,667 --> 00:06:50,021
By the end of August,
the stock was below $35.

131
00:06:50,061 --> 00:06:52,124
The cracks were no longer invisible.

132
00:06:54,176 --> 00:06:56,339
October 16, 2001.

133
00:06:56,379 --> 00:07:01,114
Enron reports a third-quarter
loss of $618 million,

134
00:07:01,154 --> 00:07:04,345
after $1.01 billion in charges.

135
00:07:04,385 --> 00:07:08,852
It also reveals a $1.2 billion
cut to shareholders’ equity,

136
00:07:08,892 --> 00:07:12,062
tied to partnerships run by its own CFO.

137
00:07:12,102 --> 00:07:15,837
October 22. The SEC
starts asking questions.

138
00:07:15,877 --> 00:07:18,426
The stock falls to around $20.

139
00:07:18,466 --> 00:07:20,637
October 24. Fastow is out.

140
00:07:20,677 --> 00:07:26,074
November 8. Enron restates its
results all the way back to 1997.

141
00:07:26,114 --> 00:07:29,299
Hundreds of millions in
reported profit, erased.

142
00:07:29,339 --> 00:07:32,695
Hundreds of millions in debt,
suddenly back on the books.

143
00:07:32,735 --> 00:07:35,000
And a trading company runs on trust.

144
00:07:35,040 --> 00:07:37,200
Partners have to believe you’ll pay.

145
00:07:37,240 --> 00:07:38,711
That trust was vanishing.

146
00:07:38,751 --> 00:07:43,619
November 9. A lifeline. Dynegy,
a Houston rival, agrees to buy Enron.

147
00:07:43,659 --> 00:07:46,019
But the more Dynegy looked,
the worse it got.

148
00:07:46,059 --> 00:07:49,505
November 28. Credit
agencies cut Enron to junk.

149
00:07:49,545 --> 00:07:51,076
Dynegy walks away.

150
00:07:51,116 --> 00:07:54,123
That day, Enron’s stock
closes at 61 cents.

151
00:07:54,873 --> 00:08:00,476
December 2, 2001. Enron files for
Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection.

152
00:08:00,516 --> 00:08:03,827
With $63.4 billion in assets,

153
00:08:03,867 --> 00:08:07,448
it was the largest bankruptcy
in American history at the time.

154
00:08:07,748 --> 00:08:10,918
From more than $90 a share, to under $1.

155
00:08:10,958 --> 00:08:12,729
In about 15 months.

156
00:08:15,079 --> 00:08:17,608
But Enron was never just a stock chart.

157
00:08:18,108 --> 00:08:23,042
Within days of the filing, about 4,000
employees in Houston were laid off.

158
00:08:23,082 --> 00:08:26,392
Many had built their
retirement on Enron stock.

159
00:08:26,432 --> 00:08:30,768
It made up roughly 60% of
the company’s 401(k) plan.

160
00:08:30,808 --> 00:08:34,833
Employees lost more than $1
billion in retirement savings.

161
00:08:35,337 --> 00:08:39,028
Investors lost tens of billions
of dollars in market value.

162
00:08:39,068 --> 00:08:41,760
And Enron’s auditor, Arthur Andersen,

163
00:08:41,800 --> 00:08:45,631
one of the five biggest accounting
firms in the world, went down with it.

164
00:08:45,671 --> 00:08:48,465
Its staff had shredded Enron documents.

165
00:08:48,505 --> 00:08:52,952
In June 2002, the firm was
convicted of obstruction of justice,

166
00:08:52,992 --> 00:08:54,740
and its business collapsed.

167
00:08:54,780 --> 00:08:57,640
The Supreme Court later
overturned the conviction.

168
00:08:57,680 --> 00:09:00,293
By then, almost nothing was left.

169
00:09:00,925 --> 00:09:02,560
Fastow pleaded guilty.

170
00:09:02,600 --> 00:09:07,113
Skilling was convicted and sentenced
to 24 years, later reduced.

171
00:09:07,153 --> 00:09:10,768
Lay was convicted too,
but died before sentencing.

172
00:09:12,954 --> 00:09:14,857
So what really destroyed Enron?

173
00:09:15,466 --> 00:09:18,151
Not one accounting method.
Not one partnership.

174
00:09:18,191 --> 00:09:19,606
Not one executive.

175
00:09:19,646 --> 00:09:21,061
It was a combination.

176
00:09:21,101 --> 00:09:24,724
Aggressive estimates that pulled
future profits into the present.

177
00:09:24,764 --> 00:09:28,411
Debt and losses moved where
investors could barely see them.

178
00:09:28,451 --> 00:09:31,082
A CFO on both sides of the deal.

179
00:09:31,122 --> 00:09:33,819
Disclosures almost no
one could understand.

180
00:09:33,859 --> 00:09:36,768
And relentless pressure to
keep the numbers climbing.

181
00:09:36,808 --> 00:09:39,843
Mark-to-market didn’t
destroy Enron on its own.

182
00:09:39,883 --> 00:09:42,887
But it helped open the gap
at the center of this story.

183
00:09:42,927 --> 00:09:46,961
The gap between looking profitable,
and actually generating cash.

184
00:09:47,711 --> 00:09:51,055
Enron spent years showing Wall
Street what it wanted to see.

185
00:09:51,095 --> 00:09:53,269
But accounting could only delay the moment

186
00:09:53,309 --> 00:09:56,104
when expectations collided with reality.

187
00:09:56,144 --> 00:09:58,637
And once investors stopped
believing the numbers,

188
00:09:58,677 --> 00:10:00,868
Enron didn’t have years left.

189
00:10:01,368 --> 00:10:02,580
It had weeks.
